## Meeting Transcript

**[09:00 AM] Director:** Welcome everyone. Reservoirs stand at 18% capacity. We have 60 days before municipal reserves hit complete depletion. We need an immediate consensus on water allocation and emergency spending.

**[09:02 AM] Dr. Aris Thorne (Hydrologist):** The situation is dire. Groundwater extraction is currently unregulated, depleting aquifers by 1.2 meters per week. If we do not immediately cap agricultural withdrawals and freeze deep-aquifer drilling, we risk permanent structural damage to the primary water table.

**[09:05 AM] Elena Rostova (Agriculture):** Capping agricultural withdrawals today will cause immediate, total collapse across 340,000 hectares of cropland. Farmers are already facing 70% yield losses. Food security must take priority. We need guaranteed irrigation allowances for the next 30 days to save the standing crop cycle.

**[09:08 AM] Marcus Vance (Infrastructure):** Respectfully, Elena, urban centers are running on 65 GPCD—half of the minimum requirement. If we prioritize cropland over municipal networks, major cities will run out of drinking water in 45 days. I propose diverting 40% of agricultural reservoir allocations into urban supply lines immediately and deploying mobile desalination fleets.

**[09:12 AM] Sophia Sterling (CFO):** Mobile desalination fleets and emergency pipelines carry a heavy price tag. Our emergency reserve drawdown is already at 15% this quarter. Authorizing immediate infrastructure spends without clear bond coverage will trigger a credit downgrade. Every dollar allocated must undergo a 72-hour ROI and necessity audit.

**[09:15 AM] Dr. Aris Thorne (Hydrologist):** We cannot wait 72 hours for fiscal audits when the water table is dropping daily. I recommend a strict 30% reduction in all sector allocations, paired with an emergency freeze on industrial withdrawals.

**[09:18 AM] Marcus Vance (Infrastructure):** I can agree to the 30% sector reduction provided municipal drinking water lines receive emergency priority from groundwater reserves.

**[09:20 AM] Elena Rostova (Agriculture):** Agriculture can accept a temporary 20% reduction only if emergency financial compensation is guaranteed for farmers facing crop loss from Sophia's reserve fund.

**[09:22 AM] Sophia Sterling (CFO):** Contingent on immediate federal grant applications, I can authorize emergency relief liquidity specifically for affected farm holdings while funding pipeline diversions.

